    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d182">

                <pb facs="0239" n="231" sameAs="frus:frus1941v07/pg231"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.61351/2389</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Messersmith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1445</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-01-22">January 22, 1941</date>.<lb/>[Received January
                        23.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Supplementing my despatch No. 1428, of
                    January 18, 1941,<anchor n="43" xml:id="d182-f1"/> and with reference to the
                    Department’s confidential telegram No. 20, January 17, 6 p.m.,<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d182-f2"/> I have the honor to report that representatives of the
                    Government met on January 20 with representatives of the sugar industry, to
                    discuss again the offer of the Export-Import Bank to finance the production
                    during 1941 of an extraordinary sugar quota of 400,000 tons. The representatives
                    of the industry were informed at the meeting that the Export-Import Bank was
                    prepared to raise the financing price from $3.25 per bag to $4.00, the other
                    terms remaining unchanged.</p>
                <p>I am enclosing a memorandum<anchor n="43" xml:id="d182-f3"/> concerning the
                    meeting, as described to the Embassy by an entirely reliable person, who was
                    present. It is obvious that, in contrast to its attitude during the first
                    meeting, the Government warmly commended the offer to the industry; and that the
                    mill owners approved the offer enthusiastically. The general impression is that
                    the objections on the part of the <hi rendition="#italic">colonos</hi><anchor
                        n="44" xml:id="d182-f4"/> were made <pb facs="0240" n="232" xml:id="pg232"/>
                    in a personal, rather than official, way. I have now been reliably informed that
                    the <hi rendition="#italic">colonos</hi> last night communicated to the
                    Government their approval of the offer. It is therefore expected that the
                    Government shortly will indicate acceptance of the offer.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George S.
                    Messersmith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
